  <section id="abstract">
    <p>
      <em>
        Education as a cultural phenomenon in the life of the foreign Circassian
        diaspora appeared almost simultaneously with its formation in the second
        half of the XIX century.
      </em>
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>UDC 946.111:379.362.9</p>
    <br />
    <p>
      <em>
        Mirzoev Aslanbek Sultanovich, Candidate of Historical Sciences, Senior
        Researcher of the Middle and New History Sector of the Institute of
        Humanitarian Studies – branch of the Federal State Budgetary Scientific
        Institution “Federal Scientific Center “Cabardian Scientific Center of
        the Russian Academy of Sciences” (ISI KBSC RAS), marzeibach@mail.ru
      </em>
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      Education as a cultural phenomenon in the life of the foreign Circassian
      diaspora appeared almost simultaneously with its formation in the second
      half of the XIX century. This phenomenon was based on several factors: the
      desire to preserve national identity in conditions of residence in a
      foreign cultural environment, the emergence of its own intelligentsia,
      which received European or Islamic education, the absence of any forms of
      national statehood. To solve the problems of preserving the language,
      traditions, national culture in the countries of residence of the
      Circassian diaspora, cultural and educational societies were created. The
      Circassian school in Kuneutre is one of the activities of the “Cherkess
      Society for the Promotion of Education and Culture”, created by a group of
      the Circassian intelligentsia in Syria led by Samugg Amin.
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      <strong>Keywords:</strong>
      <em>
        Circassian diaspora, Syria, Quneitra, Circassian school, cultural and
        educational activities.
      </em>
    </p>
  </section>
  <hr />

  <section id="article">
    <p>
      The consequence of the Russo-Caucasian War (1764–1864) was the formation
      of a large Circassian diaspora outside the Caucasus. The representatives
      of the Circassian Diaspora, who were expelled from the Caucasus in the
      Middle East, fell into another linguistic and cultural environment, faced
      with the problem of preserving their ethno-cultural identity. To preserve
      it and counteract assimilation processes, Circassian communities in their
      places of residence began to create cultural and educational societies
      dealing with the problems of studying national history, preserving the
      language, traditions and culture of Circassians.
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      Even before the collapse of the Ottoman Empire and the formation of the
      Arab states, cultural and educational activities of social, charitable and
      cultural-educational societies created by representatives of the Circassian
      community began. This became possible thanks to the political freedoms
      proclaimed after the victory of the Young Turkic Revolution of 1908.
      National and religious minorities were guaranteed civil rights, allowed the
      creation of societies and unions, and the publication of newspapers. In
      1908, the “Cherkess Society of Unity and Mutual Aid” (Khase) was opened in
      Istanbul. Since 1910, it published the Circassian newspaper Guaise,
      established contacts with the historical homeland, published books and
      gramirs written in the Kabardian dialect of the Circassian language based
      on Arabic graphics. The secretary of the society and editor-in-chief of the
      newspaper was Nuri Tsagov [1]. In 1913, he returned to his historical
      homeland - to Kabarda, where he was engaged in educational activities. In
      1917, together with the brothers Svagdev in s. The Old Fortress (now
      greenily Baksan KBR) he organizes the publication of the newspaper "Adyge
      Mc" ("Voice of Adyga") in Circassian language on an Arabic graphic basis
      [2, 13].
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      Representatives of the Circassian diaspora in the Arab countries also
      conducted active cultural and educational activities aimed at preserving
      Circassian culture, language, traditions, and national identity. In Syria,
      this activity was deployed by a group of Circassian intellectuals led by
      its leader Samug Agmin (1900–1953). He was born in Abadzeh [3] family in
      Syria. After graduating from elementary school in g. Kuneitra and Medium in
      g. Damascus, received the specialty of an agronomist in Beirut, then in the
      20-ies studied at the political economy of the Sorbon University. During
      his studies in France, he collected scientific material on the history of
      the Circassian people. As a result, he wrote several works based on them,
      from which the Introduction of the Circuses (Damascoe, 1984), “Cherkes in
      the Wars Against the Ties” (Amman, 1984) were published. In 1928-1936 he
      was the chairman of the municipality. Kuneitra, and in 1931 he was elected
      a deputy of the Syrian parliament.[4, p. 1036]. In 1927, a group of
      like-minded Samguga Amin formed the “Cherkess Society for the Promotion of
      Education and Culture”. The text of the charter of the society in the
      Ottoman language, published in the city. Kuneitra in 1928, translated into
      Russian B.N. Berezgovoye [5, c. 13-18]. "Cherkess Society of Education and
      Unity" (literal translation of the name of the organization from the
      Circassian language - A. M.) set as its task to carry out comprehensive
      activities aimed at preserving the native language and customs; to promote
      the development of national literature;to seek from local authorities and
      representatives of the French colonial administration to grant the
      Circassian community of autonomy in the Qunetra district. The activities of
      the company were headed by the Central Committee, consisting of 9 people
      and located in the city. Kuneitra. In the cities and villages of compact
      residence of the Circassians, city and rural branches of the society were
      created, headed by the elected leaders (thamada) and subordinate to the
      Central Committee. The general meeting (congress) of the company was held
      annually on May 10 in the year. The Kuneitre, which approved the annual
      plan of activities, income and expenses of the organization. May 10 was
      declared the day of the national holiday [5, p. 16].
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      In Damascus in 1929, under the editorship of Balague Naubatuka, a Circassian
      primer was released, for which the alphabet was used on a Latin basis [6].
      The publication of pamphlets and school textbooks in Circassian began. The
      new society was established by the publication of the newspaper "Marj"
      (from October 7, 1927 to 1932), the editor-in-chief of which was Hagur
      Taryk Mumtaz. The newspaper "Marj" was published in four languages:
      Circassian, Turkish, Arabic, French. Accordingly, it had four pages. The
      newspaper published materials on the history, culture and various problems
      of the Circassian people in Syria and other countries. The issues of the
      newspaper were sent to the Circassian communities of Transjordan, Egypt and
      Turkey [7]. On the basis of the Circassian Society for the Promotion of
      Education and Culture, the Circassian Literary Union was created, under the
      chairmanship of Salim Zakaria, various committees, sports society. The
      activity of the society lasted until 1932, after which it was closed for
      political reasons [8, c. 119].
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      In 1933, with the support of the French administration, with which Samgug
      Amin cooperated, in the city. The Circassian National School of Cuneutre
      was opened to educate children from all over the district [7, c. 119]. The
      school was held both at the expense of public funds and on private
      donations [9]. Samgug Amin and other patriots opened stores, the income
      from which went to finance the school. Soldiers of the Circassian regiment
      of the French armed forces in Syria sacrificed part of their salary for the
      maintenance of the school and the publication of the Marj newspaper [7].
      Teachers from various Circassian areas of Syria were invited to teach at
      the school [8, c. 119]. These are Amin Samugg, Yahya Schharkas, Akif
      Hunagu, Fakhri Haju, Ismail Anzor, Rufat Gutoko, Sheikh-Tagir Jatao,
      Muhamad Thakayo, Hamad-Ali Ishako, Yahya Hatko, Rajab and Harun Blanao,
      Abdul-Latif Udzhyu, Adib Shagh. Among the teachers were women: Charlotte
      Gutuko and Satani Libzu. Among these teachers were poets: Samguh Amin, Kat
      Ahmad, Tsai Ibrahim, Shapsug Sheikh-Saied, Hazhu Fakhri, Harun Baco
      (Blanao) [9; 10, p. 267-268]. The first principal of the school was Rahfate
      Gutoko, but he died in April 1933. Akif Hunagu worked as the then director
      of the school [9]. In addition to educational subjects, it taught:
      Circassian language, folk customs, songs and dances. The children tried to
      instill a spirit of patriotism and love for their historical homeland [8,
      p. 119]. Every day classes at school began with the performance of the
      Circassian anthem by the students, the words and music for which was
      composed by Samgug Amin [7; 10]. According to the program of study from
      grade 1 to 3, teaching was conducted only in the Circassian language. From
      4 to 5, the Arabic was connected. Those who taught Arabic were Arabs, they
      were provided by the state, all other teachers were Circassians. From grade
      5, the French language was connected. The French teacher was Charlotte
      Gutoko, who was educated in France. Arabic and French were studied as a
      foreign language. All the students of the school were Circassians. The
      letters for the Circassian language were Latin. The letters (font) for
      printing were made in the school itself of wood. The books were samizoted.
      Everything was done by the teachers. In addition to the school, it was
      planned to open schools in three specialties: biological, trade and
      economic, craft (industrial). Those who graduated were to be sent to
      continue their studies at universities in Syria and other countries. The
      purpose of the school was the founders saw the creation of the Circasians
      in the national spirit, instilling in them the knowledge of customs,
      folklore, history. The students of the school, in their view, had,
      figuratively speaking, to become “seeds for sowing” – they saw future
      ideologues and practitioners of returning their people to their historical
      homeland. But for this they had to be prepared ideologically and
      well-educated [9].
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      Students, boys and girls, wore national clothes. The school’s music
      orchestra was recruited from the students. National dances were taught at
      the school. Every year, cultural and sports festivals were held, where
      students competed in reading poems in the Circassian language, knowledge of
      folklore, performed by national songs and dances. There was a school
      theater that staged small performances in Circassian. Much attention was
      paid to the physical training of students, sports festivals were held, at
      which competitions were held in various sports disciplines. Two football
      teams of Circassians living in the Golan - "Yamlidzh" and "Nucleb" [9].
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      Every year in the competition among primary schools, the Circassian school
      took 1 place. Teachers worked with great enthusiasm. The school worked
      until 1943. The reason for the closure was several factors. One of them is
      the beginning of the Second World War. In Syria, declared a military zone,
      a state of emergency was established. The territory of the Middle East was
      the scene of hostilities, and French military units were divided into
      supporters of General de Gaulle, the leader of the French resistance and
      supporters of the French collaborators - the "Sichist" who embarked on the
      path of cooperation with fascist Germany. The second factor is the
      strengthening of the pro-Arab group of Circassians, the number of
      supporters of which increased in the early 30-ies. It was headed by major
      landowners from the Circassian clans of the Bag and Duguzh. “This group,”
      as A noted in its work. Kushkhabiyev maintained close ties with Arab
      nationalist parties and organizations. Emphasizing the religious community
      with the Arabs and Syrian unity, they opposed the creation of Circassian
      autonomy and called on their compatriots to support the Arab liberation
      movement, supported by Arab nationalists fighting for the abolition of the
      French mandate and the creation of an independent Syrian Arab state” [8,
      118].
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      In September 1936, a treaty was signed between Syria and France,
      recognizing independence as the first. The mandate was subject to
      cancellation within three years from the date of its ratification. The
      strengthening of the Arab liberation movement was the result of the Syrian
      government's offensive on the rights of ethnic and religious minorities. On
      9 September 1936, there was an armed clash between two Circassian youth
      groups: supporters of the Samguga group and the organization of the
      pro-Arab orientation "Iron Shirts". There were losses on both sides. The
      incident was used by the Syrian national government as a pretext to accuse
      the “Cherkesian Society for the Promotion of Education and Culture” in
      collaboration with the French. In this regard, the further activities of
      the society were prohibited [8, p. 120). He was removed from the post of
      chairman of the municipal council. Kuneitra Amin Samgug.
    </p>
    <br />
    <p>
      In 1937, Samgug Amin and his associates formed a new organization "Jolan",
      which aimed to facilitate the mass return of Circassians to the Caucasus.
      Representatives of this organization appealed to the Soviet Embassy in
      Paris with a request to grant Syrian Circassians the right to
      repatriation, but received a negative response, after which the activities
      of the "Jolen" was suspended. Amin Samugg retired from the political
      struggle, continued only scientific activity and teaching at school, until
      its closure in 1943.
    </p>
